WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.283
What models actually take from examples.

2
00:00:02.563 --> 00:00:08.493
Research on few-shot prompting is humbling, and worth knowing because it changes how you choose examples.

3
00:00:08.773 --> 00:00:19.184
Min and colleagues (2022) replaced the labels in classification examples with random ones and found accuracy barely dropped, across 12 models.

4
00:00:19.464 --> 00:00:26.781
What mattered more was that the examples showed the set of possible labels, the kind of input text, and the format of the answer.

5
00:00:27.061 --> 00:00:30.752
In other words, models lean heavily on examples as a template.

6
00:00:31.032 --> 00:00:42.147
That is good news for format control and bad news for accidents: if every example summary opens with “Overall,” or runs exactly three sentences, your outputs will too.

7
00:00:42.427 --> 00:00:48.123
Call this format leakage, and prevent it with deliberate variety in everything you don’t want copied.

8
00:00:48.403 --> 00:00:51.368
Order matters too, at least on the models of that era.

9
00:00:51.648 --> 00:01:05.771
Lu and colleagues (2021) showed that simply reordering the same few examples could move a model from near state-of-the-art accuracy to near chance, and that a good order for one model did not transfer to another.

10
00:01:06.051 --> 00:01:16.696
Zhao and colleagues (2021) traced part of this instability to biases toward answers that appear near the end of the prompt or appear often in the examples.

11
00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:23.909
These studies used GPT-3-era models, and today’s instruction-tuned models are generally more robust.

12
00:01:24.189 --> 00:01:34.323
But the engineering conclusions hold: balance your labels, vary the surface features, and when results matter, test the same prompt with the examples in a shuffled order.

13
00:01:34.603 --> 00:01:36.885
If the score moves, your prompt is fragile.
